# This app is a client-side-routed SPA with no server-side rendering --
# Ember's router only ever runs once index.html's JS bundle has loaded and
# booted, so it only ever handles a route if that route change happened
# *after* the app was already running (an in-app <LinkTo>, a
# browser-history back/forward). A hard refresh or a direct visit to any
# deep URL (e.g. /signed-in/admin/chats) instead goes straight to Netlify,
# which -- correctly, from its point of view -- looks for a real file at
# that path, finds none (there is no dist/signed-in/admin/chats; that path
# only ever existed as a client-side route), and 404s before Ember ever
# gets a chance to run.
#
# The fix isn't to pre-render each route into a matching directory
# structure -- that's static-site generation, which this app doesn't use
# and doesn't need. It's to tell Netlify to serve index.html (status 200,
# not a redirect) for any path that doesn't match a real file in dist/,
# so the browser's URL bar keeps the original deep-link URL but the
# *content* served is the same app shell every route already boots from.
# Ember's router then reads that URL itself and renders the matching
# route client-side, running the exact same guards
# (`signed-in.js` -> login, `admin.js` -> not-found, etc.) it already runs
# for in-app navigation -- there's nothing else to implement for the
# "redirect to home when auth/permissions fail" behavior the user wants on
# a hard refresh; it's already there, this just lets it actually run.
#
# This file lives in public/, not dist/, because dist/ is build output
# (gitignored, regenerated on every build) -- anything in public/ is
# copied into dist/ verbatim by the build (see public/robots.txt showing
# up at dist/robots.txt already), so this ships automatically whether the
# build runs locally or on Netlify.
/*  /index.html  200
